<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # The Local Farm Stand – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 67%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Local Farm Stand</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Need Two Papayas, Please.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Create basic informational leaflets for the event. Interactive: Write simple group summaries of chapters read in class.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can create an informational leaflet describing farm stand items and prices for customers (e.g., "We have apples for one dollar and bananas for two dollars."). Can write a simple summary of a story (e.g., "The story is about a farm stand. They sell fruits."). Can tell classmates about the farm stand (e.g., "Come to our farm stand for fruit!").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification. Activates prior knowledge and introduces the lesson's context.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards or pictures of various fruits and vegetables from the farm stand (e.g., papaya, banana, mango, tomato, lettuce). Students quickly list (write) the names they see in their notebooks. Then, the teacher asks "Which ones are fruits? Which ones are vegetables?" to categorize.</p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Review vocabulary: mango, pineapple, guava, tomato, pepper, lettuce, papaya, banana, fruit, vegetable.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a picture of a "papaya" and writes "Papaya: $3.00 each" clearly on the whiteboard, enunciating "papaya" and emphasizing the /p/ sound. Then, holds up a picture of "bananas" and writes "Bananas: $2.00 a bunch" on the board, emphasizing the /b/ sound in "banana" and segmenting its syllables: "ba-na-na".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'papaya' spelled with a 'p' or a 'b'?" (Elicit 'p').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How many syllables does 'banana' have? Let's clap it: ba-na-na." (Elicit 'three').</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to the Scenario. Students observe a model text for their writing task.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher displays a simple, clear example of an informational leaflet for a farm stand on a large poster or projected screen. The leaflet contains item names, prices, and short descriptions.</p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Example Leaflet Text:</b> "Welcome to Our Sunny Farm Stand! We have fresh fruits and vegetables every day. Delicious Papayas: $3.00 each. Sweet Bananas: $2.00 per bunch. Juicy Mangoes: $1.50 each. Do you need any fresh tomatoes? They are only $1.00 each. We also have some tasty cheese for $4.00 a block. Come and buy some healthy food!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Focus on grammar: "How much is...?", "some" and "any" with countable/non-countable nouns.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Vocabulary: mango, pineapple, guava, tomato, pepper, lettuce, papaya, banana, fruit, farm, egg, cheese, bread, vegetable, milk, sugar, salt, water, market, cashier, price, need, buy, sell, cost, ask, choose, want, give, get, cheap, fresh, ripe, delicious, healthy, a lot, a few, some, many.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students can understand and extract specific information about products and prices from a written informational leaflet.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the leaflet silently, then the teacher asks gist and specific questions, guiding them to write down the answers.</p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What fruits does the Sunny Farm Stand sell?" (Students write: Papayas, Bananas, Mangoes).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How much is one papaya?" (Students write: $3.00).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do they have any cheese? How much is it?" (Students write: Yes, $4.00).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Focus on accuracy in Writing. Guided practice of target language and sentence structures.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Writing sentences using polite requests ("Can you help me, please?"), WH-questions ("How much is...?"), and "some"/"any" with farm stand vocabulary. Focus on adjectives like "fresh," "ripe," "delicious."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion):</b> The teacher provides sentences with blanks related to farm stand items and prices. Students fill in the blanks using words from a given word bank (e.g., "some", "any", "papayas", "dollars", "fresh").<br>
                        - Example: "Do you have _____ (some/any) apples?" "I would like _____ (some/any) water, please." "We have _____ (fresh/cheap) mangoes." "How much is this _____ (fruit/price)?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Writing (Sentence Building):</b> The teacher gives students specific prompts (e.g., "banana / $2.00 / sweet", "guava / $1.00 / ripe", "milk / no / any") and asks them to write complete sentences or short questions based on the model leaflet. <br>
                        - Example prompts: "papaya, $3.00, delicious" -> Students write: "We have delicious papayas for $3.00." or "How much is a delicious papaya? It is $3.00."<br>
                        - Example prompts: "eggs, some, fresh" -> Students write: "We have some fresh eggs."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Peer Check (Written Feedback):</b> Students exchange their written sentences from activity 2 with a partner. They check each other's sentences for correct spelling of vocabulary (e.g., mango, pineapple, tomato, papaya), proper use of "some/any," and clear sentence structure. Partners provide polite, written feedback (e.g., "Good sentence!", "Check spelling for 'delicious'.").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students use the language to perform a meaningful communicative task in a realistic scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Writing - Creating an Informational Farm Stand Leaflet.</p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students) to create their own informational leaflet for an imaginary farm stand.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each leaflet must include:</li>
                        <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A creative name for their farm stand.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">At least 5 different farm stand items (fruits, vegetables, other products like bread, cheese, eggs).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A price for each item.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">At least two descriptive adjectives (e.g., fresh, ripe, delicious, healthy, cheap).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">At least one "How much is...?" question or "Do you have any...?" statement.</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">At least one polite phrase (e.g., "Welcome!", "Please come!").</li>
                        </ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students will draw simple pictures of their items on the leaflet to make it visually appealing.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. Divide the class into small groups (3-4 students).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. Provide each group with a large piece of paper or cardboard and colored pencils/markers.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. Explain the task: "You are going to create a leaflet for your own farm stand. Write the name of your farm stand, list items with prices and descriptions. Use the vocabulary and grammar we practiced today."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">4. Circulate among groups, providing support, checking for correct use of grammar (some/any, how much), spelling, and encouraging creativity.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">5. Each group then briefly presents their leaflet to the class, reading out their items and prices, practicing clear pronunciation (e.g., /p/ in papaya, /b/ in banana).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">12-15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment to check learning and provide feedback.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their group-created informational leaflets.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (for the written leaflet):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Information:</b> Are the farm stand items and prices clearly written and easy to understand?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Does the leaflet use at least 5 words from the farm stand vocabulary list (e.g., mango, papaya, fresh, delicious, cost, sell)?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Grammar:</b> Does the leaflet include correct use of polite requests, WH-questions ("How much is...?"), and "some"/"any" constructions?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sentence Structure & Spelling:</b> Are sentences basic but coherent, and is spelling generally correct for target vocabulary?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> (Observed during group work) Did all group members contribute to the creation of the leaflet?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. What is one new word you wrote in your leaflet today?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. Can you write a price for a fruit you know? (e.g., "A mango is _____ dollars.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Review of the lesson and planning for future learning.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. What was easy about writing your farm stand leaflet?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. What was difficult? How can you improve your writing next time?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students successfully create informational leaflets with prices and descriptions? Were they able to include at least 5 items and their prices with some descriptive language?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were students able to incorporate "some/any" and "How much is...?" accurately in their written work?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What grammatical structures or vocabulary words need more practice for the next writing task? Was the CEFR A1.3 level appropriate for the writing demand?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can I better scaffold the writing process for students who struggled with sentence construction or vocabulary recall? What adjustments are needed for differentiation?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "In our next lesson, we will integrate all our skills by using these farm stand leaflets in a market simulation. You will practice selling and buying, and summarize your experiences and the information you gather from other 'farm stands'."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>